WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:05.660
Speaker 1: Let's go back to the phones, by
which I mean our discord voice server, which

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:12.259
you can find at David Pakman dot com slash
discord, there are so many people hoping to

00:00:12.259 --> 00:00:13.259
get on.

00:00:13.259 --> 00:00:14.889
Let's talk to some of them.

00:00:14.889 --> 00:00:18.480
Why don't we go to Ryan from Dallas, Texas.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.330
Ryan, you're on the air.

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:21.810
Hi, David.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:22.810
Can you hear me?

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:23.810
I can hear you fine.

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:25.050
Speaker 6: Hi there.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:29.660
So, you know, I'm a I'm a young black man,
and, you know, I'm not I don't really consider

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:31.930
myself to be too liberal or conservative.

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:35.020
I kind of find myself in the middle.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:40.290
And I don't really like to cry wolf for every
single issue with the minority, like to assess

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:42.440
it based on case by case basis.

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:48.110
But my question for you is, you know, Jim
Crow, only fifty six years ago and there were

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:52.900
there really wasn't anything significantly
done to try to uplift, you know, and compensate

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.490
the black community for those years of oppression.

00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:56.490
Yes.

00:00:56.490 --> 00:01:00.700
And my question to you is, what do you think
should have been done and at the time and

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.160
what do you think we could do now?

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:08.289
Speaker 1: Well, the problem at the time was
that, as we have very much learned, simply

00:01:08.289 --> 00:01:13.179
changing the rules on paper to outlaw certain
types of discrimination.

00:01:13.179 --> 00:01:18.249
It, number one, doesn't actually eliminate
the discrimination right away.

00:01:18.249 --> 00:01:23.380
And number two, it doesn't actually set people
up on an even playing field.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:26.400
I've used the monopoly analogy before.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:31.200
If a new player enters a Monopoly game after
a few people have been playing for an hour

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.899
on paper, you can say everybody's playing
by the same rules.

00:01:33.899 --> 00:01:37.460
But all the properties have been purchased
and the bank already has relationships with

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:38.759
the existing players, etc..

00:01:38.759 --> 00:01:40.009
OK, that's the analogy.

00:01:40.009 --> 00:01:42.530
So I think that was the missed opportunity
then.

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:48.039
Now, I don't know if you're getting towards
the idea of of reparations or not.

00:01:48.039 --> 00:01:51.240
My thoughts on this and people people get
very angry with me.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:52.789
Some people get angry with me when I say it.

00:01:52.789 --> 00:01:56.590
But this is an issue I've looked into to a
significant degree.

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:58.189
I don't claim to be an expert on it.

00:01:58.189 --> 00:02:05.189
I have not seen evidence that sort of throwing
money at people is actually going to solve

00:02:05.189 --> 00:02:11.569
the issues that remain as vestiges of the
slavery and Jim Crow era.

00:02:11.569 --> 00:02:18.010
Throwing money at people won't solve a lot
of the geographic structural issues that we

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:24.659
have in terms of neighborhoods and public
transit and a lot of other different things.

00:02:24.659 --> 00:02:30.209
It won't, at least not in any direct way solve
some of the education inequalities that exist.

00:02:30.209 --> 00:02:38.290
So I am open to the idea of a reparative process,
but I'm more interested in seeing how it can

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:42.959
go to structural change rather than necessarily
like tossing money at people.

00:02:42.959 --> 00:02:44.549
Speaker 6: Right.

00:02:44.549 --> 00:02:46.050
I completely agree.

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:50.500
I definitely think that there needs to be
a new basic standard for, you know, I mean,

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:51.849
simply simple stuff like apartments.

00:02:51.849 --> 00:02:56.019
You know, a lot of these people are living
in these apartments that are just or I mean,

00:02:56.019 --> 00:02:57.319
just horrid apartments.

00:02:57.319 --> 00:03:01.430
I used to live in some terrible apartments,
rat infested when I was when I was little.

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:02.430
So.

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:06.190
And also schools, you know, they're so underfunded
and you have these schools in the suburbs

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:07.840
buying, you know, new stadiums.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.340
So I'm completely on board with that.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:11.590
Speaker 1: Now, let me ask you this.

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:14.719
You said that you're not super left or right.

00:03:14.719 --> 00:03:17.189
What's an issue on which you consider yourself
a centrist?

00:03:17.189 --> 00:03:19.870
Like, is there a social issue where you're
a centrist?

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:25.011
Speaker 6: For instance, you know, this bill
that came out about the Equality Act, you

00:03:25.011 --> 00:03:31.819
know, I completely agree that there needs
to be some formal, you know, regulation on

00:03:31.819 --> 00:03:35.500
not being discriminated because of your gender
identity and things like that.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:37.280
But like, I don't agree with that.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.680
You know, that they should be able to play
in the sport that they identify with.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:45.140
I guess I guess in my opinion, that's being
neutral, but I don't know.

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:46.140
Speaker 1: Hold on.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:49.510
Does that apply to all sports or only certain
sports?

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:53.769
Speaker 6: I honestly haven't thought about
it that much.

00:03:53.769 --> 00:04:02.189
You know, I guess in my mind, I would initially
think about the more physically, you know,

00:04:02.189 --> 00:04:07.590
where you know where where your physical stature
is more important and significant.

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:12.150
But I guess I guess maybe it's just a defense
mechanism because I want people to actually

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:17.260
listen to me versus just making assumptions
about me because, you know, I'm a young black

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:18.260
guy.

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:21.660
Speaker 1: So it almost sounds like, in a
sense, you are taking some of these positions

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:25.420
to posture as something other than a liberal.

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:32.161
Speaker 6: And maybe I maybe that could be
the case.

00:04:32.161 --> 00:04:33.500
Yeah, I would think about that.

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:37.410
I definitely don't think that I'm I'm more
liberal than conservative for sure.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:38.600
Speaker 1: You don't think so?

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:39.600
Or you are more liberal?

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:40.600
Speaker 6: No, no.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:42.240
I am definitely more liberal than conservative.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:43.240
Speaker 1: I see.

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:44.240
Yeah, that's coming through.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:45.240
That's coming through.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:47.160
All right, my friend, thank you for the call.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:48.160
I appreciate it.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:49.160
Thank you.

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:50.160
All right.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:51.160
Take care.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:54.170
We are hearing from people via discord at
David Pakman dotcom slash discord.

00:04:54.170 --> 00:05:03.430
The waiting lobby is the place to signal your
interest in getting on air with me.

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:08.030
Why don't we go next to let's go to Annette.

00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:09.590
Annette, you are on the air.

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:19.080
Annette, that you can on mute and then we'll
be able to chat.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:35.590
Annette, I 
would love to speak to you, but I can't if

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:52.350
you mute yourself and I would give you just
this one last opportunity, of course, with

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:54.340
peace and love.

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:59.850
All right, no, Annette, that's really too
bad.

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:12.190
That's a shame

